      "year": 2019,
      "abstract": "Is the world crumbing? In Enlightenment Now: The Case for Reason, Science, Humanism, and Progress, Steven Pinker invites us to revisit our defeatist assumptions about the state of the world, often influenced by our individual and collective psychological biases. Building on a rich historical approach, Pinker shows that the world is doing much better than ever considering several dimensions such as health, wealth, inequality, peace, safety, knowledge and democracy. There is no doubt in Steven Pinker\u2019s mind; this progress is the result of the Enlightenment values of reason, science and humanism, as opposed to authority, tradition and dogma. According to him, today is the most wonderful time to be born. This book is of great interest to organizational studies considering that in recent years organization studies have come much closer to \u2018wicked\u2019 problems in and around organizations. This dynamic has translated into significant interest from organization scholars in topics that address such societal issues. Over the past decade or so, Organization Studies has for instance published seven special issues on societal issues and/or their organizational implications; moral agency in organizations (2006, Vol. 27, Is. 3), climate change (2012, Vol. 33, Is. 11), social movements and corporations (2013, Vol. 34, Is. 5\u20136), the dark side of organizations (2014, Vol. 35, Is. 2), organizational and institutional trust (2015, Vol.36, Is. 9), resistance in and around organizations (2017, Vol. 38, Is. 9), and finally, inequalities and institutions (2018, Vol. 39, Is. 9). Organization Studies is no exception insofar as this trend is also visible in most other journals covering our field. The path may have been long and turbulent, but it seems like our research community has seriously taken a \u2018social\u2019 \u2013 or perhaps a \u2018sustainability\u2019 \u2013 turn. Steven Pinker is a psychology professor at Harvard, researching visual cognition and psycholinguistics. His book Enlightenment Now examines a seemingly mundane but crucial question: Is the world falling apart? Extreme poverty, climate change, inequalities, wars, genocides, refugee crisis, the world seems to be getting worse despite reaching levels of knowledge, technology, wisdom, innovation and wealth that humanity has rarely witnessed. This is at least what the intelligentsia tends to convey. Put in Pinker\u2019s words, \u2018both the left and the right . . . include pessimism about the way the world is heading, cynicism about the institutions of modernity, and an inability to conceive of a higher purpose in anything other than religion\u2019 (p. xvii). What if the world is doing much better? With his long-term historical perspective, attentive to psycho-cognitive processes, Pinker argues that despite the generalized pessimism and \u2018progressophobia\u2019 (p. 39), our world is getting better on a deluge of metrics. 884038OSS0010.1177/0170840619884038Organization StudiesBook Reviews book-review2019",
